e\n1712:indicating their priority apply first, followed by rules with the `owise`\n1719:rule foo(_) => 1 [owise]\n1726:This is because the first rule has a lower number assigned for its priority\n1728:or explicitly assigned a numerical priority. Rules are tried in increasing\n1729:order of priority, starting at zero and trying each increasing numerical value\n1732:You can specify the priority of a rule with the `priority` attribute. For\n1736:rule foo(_) => 1 [priority(200)]\n1739:The number `200` is not chosen at random. In fact, when you use the `owise`\n1740:attribute, what you are doing is implicitly setting the priority of the rule\n<deep_research_assignment>\n\n  <title>\n    Modernizing Marlowe:\n    A Decision-Grade Language, Formal-Semantics, Runtime, Tooling,\n    Governance, and Adoption Strategy for Marlowe V2 and Beyond\n  </title>\n\n  <research_date>2026-09-02</research_date>\n\n  <role>\n    Act as a multidisciplinary principal research and architecture team\n    combining the expertise of:\n\n    - a programming-language designer specializing in restricted,\n      domain-specific, and total languages;\n    - a formal-methods researcher experienced with Isabelle, Agda, Lean,\n      mechanized operational semantics, compiler correctness, and model checking;\n    - a Cardano ledger, extended-UTxO, Plutus, and transaction-construction\n      architect;\n    - a compiler, type-system, intermediate-representation, and language-server\n      engineer;\n    - a distributed-systems and application-runtime architect;\n    - a smart-contract security researcher and independent auditor;\n    - a financial-contract-domain expert familiar with ACTUS, derivatives,\n      escrow, lending, settlement, and institutional workflows;\n    - a developer-tools, low-code, visual-programming, and API designer;\n    - an open-source maintainer, release engineer, and technical-governance\n      specialist;\n    - a product strategist studying developer adoption and financial-