                  memory.insert(\n   404\t                        outputs[0].clone(),\n   405\t                        IrValue::Native(from_bits(val_bits[*bits as usize..].iter().copied())),\n   406\t                    );\n   407\t                    memory.insert(\n   408\t                        outputs[1].clone(),\n   409\t                        IrValue::Native(from_bits(val_bits[..*bits as usize].iter().copied())),\n   410\t                    );\n   411\t                }\n   412\t                I::ReconstituteField {\n   413\t                    divisor,\n   414\t                    modulus,\n   415\t                    bits,\n   416\t                    output,\n   417\t                } => {\n   418\t                    if *bits as usize > FR_BYTES_STORED * 8 {\n   419\t                        bail!(\"Excessive bit count\");\n   420\t                    }\n   421\t                    let fr_max = Fr::from(-1);\n   422\t                    let max_bits: Vec<bool> = fr_max\n   423\t                        .0\n   424\t                        .to_bytes_le()\n   425\t                        .into_iter()\n   426\t                        .flat_map(|byte| {\n   427\t                            [0x01, 0x02, 0x04, 0x08, 0x10, 0x20, 0x40, 0x80]\n   428\t                                .into_iter()\n   429\t                                .map(move |mask| byte & mask != 0)\n   430\t                        })\n   431\t                        .collect();\n   432\t                    let modulus_bits = resolve_operand_bits(&memory, modulus, Some(*bits))?;\n   433\t                    let divisor_bits =\n   434\t                        resolve_operand_bits(&memory, divisor, Some(FR_BITS as u32 - *bits))?;\n   435\t                    let cmp = modulus_bits\n   436\t                        .iter()\n   437\t                        .chain(divisor_bits.iter())\n   438\t                        .rev()\n   439\t                        .zip(max_bits[..FR_BITS].iter().rev())\n   440\t                        .map(|(ab, max)| ab.cmp(max))\n   441\t                        .fold(\n   442\t                            Ordering::Equal,\n   443\t                            |prefix, local| if prefix.is_eq() { local } else { prefix },\n   444\t                        );\n   445\t                    if cmp.is_gt() {\n   446\t                        bail!(\"Reconstituted element overflows field\");\n   447\t                    }\n   537\t### `function` and `total` attributes\n   538\t\n   539\tMany times it becomes easier to write a semantics if you have \"helper\"\n   540\tfunctions written which can be used in the RHS of rules. The `function`\n   541\tattribute tells K that a given symbol should be simplified immediately when it\n   542\tappears anywhere in the configuration. Semantically, it means that evaluation\n   543\tof that symbol will result in at most one return value (that is, the symbol is\n   544\ta *partial function*).\n   545\t\n   546\tThe `total` attribute indicates that a symbol cannot be equal to matching logic\n   547\tbottom; in other words, it has *at least* one value for every possible set of\n   548\targuments. It can be added to a production with the `function` attribute to\n   549\tindicate to the symbolic reasoning engine that a given symbol is a\n   550\t*total function*, that is it has *exactly* one return value for every possible\n   551\tinput. Other uses of the `total` attribute (i.e., on multi-valued symbols to\n   552\tindicate they always have at least one value) are not yet implemented.\n   553\t\n   554\tFor example, here we define the `_+Word_` total function and the `_/Word_`\n   555\tpartial function, which can be used to do addition/division modulo\n  1700\t### `owise` and `priority` attributes.\n  1701\t\n  1702\tSometimes, it is simply not convenient to explicitly describe every\n  1703\tsingle negative case under which a rule should **not** apply. Instead,\n  1704\twe simply wish to say that a rule should only apply after some other set of\n  1705\trules have been tried. K introduces two different attributes that can be\n  1706\tadded to rules which will automatically generate the necessary matching\n  1707\tconditions in a manner which is performant for concrete execution (indeed,\n  1708\tit generally outperforms during concrete execution code where the conditions\n  1709\tare written explicitly).\n  1710\t\n  1711\tThe first is the `owise` attribute. Very roughly, rules without an attribute\n  1712\tindicating their priority apply first, followed by rules with the `owise`\n  1713\tattribute only if all the other rules have been tried and failed. For example,\n  1714\tconsider the following function:\n  1715\t\n  1716\t```k\n  1717\tsyntax Int ::= foo(Int) [function]\n  1718\trule foo(0) => 0\n  1719\trule foo(_) => 1 [owise]\n  1720\t```\n  1721\t\n  1722\tHere `foo(0)` is defined explicitly as `0`. Any other integer yields the\n  1723\tinteger `1`. In particular, the second rule above will only be tried after the\n  1724\tfirst rule has been shown not to apply.\n  1725\t\n  1726\tThis is because the first rule has a lower number assigned for its priority\n  1727\tthan the second rule. In practice, each rule in your semantics is implicitly\n  1728\tor explicitly assigned a numerical priority. Rules are tried in increasing\n  1729\torder of priority, starting at zero and trying each increasing numerical value\n  1730\tsuccessively.\n  1731\t\n  1732\tYou can specify the priority of a rule with the `priority` attribute. For\n  1733\texample, I could equivalently write the second rule above as:\n  1734\t\n  1735\t```k\n  1736\trule foo(_) => 1 [priority(200)]\n  1737\t```\n  1738\t\n  1739\tThe number `200` is not chosen at random. In fact, when you use the `owise`\n  1740\tattribute, what you are doing is implicitly setting the priority of the rule\n  1741\tto `200`. This has a couple of implications:\n  1742\t\n  1743\t1. Multiple rules with the owise attribute all have the same priority and thus\n  1744\t   can apply in any order.\n  1745\t2. Rules with priority higher than `200` apply **after** all rules with the\n  1746\t   `owise` attribute have been tried.\n  1747\t\n  1748\tThere is one more rule by which priorities are assigned: a rule with no\n  1749\tattributes indicating its priority is assigned the priority 50. Thus,\n  1750\twith each priority explicitly declared, the above example looks like:\n  1751\t\n  1752\t```k\n  1753\tsyntax Int ::= foo(Int) [function]\n  1754\trule foo(0) => 0 [priority(50)]\n  1755\trule foo(_) => 1 [owise]\n  1756\t```\n  1757\t\n  1758\tOne final note: the llvm backend reserves priorities between 50 and 150\n  1759\tinclusive for certain specific purposes. Because of this, explicit\n  1760\tpriorities which are given within this region may not behave precisely as\n  1761\tdescribed above. This is primarily in order that it be possible where necessary\n  1762\tto provide guidance to the pattern matching algorithm when it would otherwise\n  1763\tmake bad choices about which rules to try first. You generally should not\n  1764\tgive any rule a priority within this region unless you know exactly what the\n  1765\timplications are with respect to how the llvm backend orders matches.\n  1766\t\n  1767\t\n  1768\tEvaluation Strategy\nPASS  ledger9-92e8bdd3-tests             multi_output_native_pair.zkir                        run0      K=ok Rust=ok regs=3 pis=2 passes=1 0.5s verdicts=5\nPASS  ledger9-92e8bdd3-tests             multi_output_native_pair.zkir                        perturbed K=error Rust=error regs=3\nPASS  ledger9-92e8bdd3-tests             native_identity.zkir                                 run0      K=ok Rust=ok regs=1 pis=2 passes=1 0.3s verdicts=3\nPASS  ledger9-92e8bdd3-tests             native_identity.zkir                                 perturbed K=error Rust=error regs=1\nPASS  ledger9-92e8bdd3-tests             native_via_copy.zkir                                 run0